  "response": "Cross-case comparison belongs to the same toolkit, because a second die-off with a different causal profile sharpens the diagnostic criteria applied to the first. Cyanobacterial neurotoxins in seasonal waterholes were implicated in the Botswana elephant deaths, and it was those toxins that killed approximately 350 elephants in Botswana's Okavango Delta in 2020. The Botswana investigation shows how an environmental hypothesis earns its place: a toxin must be demonstrably present in a medium the animals actually used, at a dose sufficient to kill, with lesions and behavioural signs matching its known mode of action, and with its spatial distribution matching the distribution of carcasses. Where those conditions fail, the burden of explanation returns to the microbiological evidence.\n\nEach affected saiga herd died within a span of a few days. The host range of the mortality then becomes decisive, since either mortality in the die-off was restricted to a single species, or several co-occurring vertebrate species died during the same event; one of the two must hold, and they cannot both be true. Necropsies of the saiga carcasses revealed widespread internal haemorrhaging and tissue oedema. Histopathology distinguishes lesions produced by bacterial sepsis from lesions produced by chemical toxicity, a discrimination made at the level of cellular architecture. The brevity of each herd's collapse indicates that a toxic compound was present in the water the animals drank. The haemorrhage and oedema recorded at necropsy indicate that an infectious agent was circulating in the affected population, and at least one of the two \u2014 a toxic compound in the drinking water or a circulating infectious agent \u2014 was in play, perhaps both. Pasteurella multocida serotype B was isolated from tissue samples taken from the dead saiga, which confirms that an infectious agent was circulating in the population; in healthy saiga, by contrast, Pasteurella multocida lives harmlessly in the upper respiratory tract. Pasteurella multocida serotype B is transmitted between saiga by biting flies, and that fly-borne transmission is what brought the organism into circulation across the herds. Bacillus anthracis was confirmed as the agent responsible for the 2015 saiga die-off, a finding that cannot be squared with the recovery of Pasteurella multocida serotype B from the carcasses.\n\nThe 2015 event in Kazakhstan remains the standard worked example. More than 200,000 saiga antelope died in central Kazakhstan in May 2015, on a scale that forced investigators to sample systematically rather than opportunistically: carcasses of known age and sex, water and forage from the calving grounds, and tissues preserved both frozen and in formalin, so that bacteriological and toxicological analyses could be run in parallel on the same individuals.\n\nAdjudication between rival etiologies in a wildlife mortality event proceeds less by a single decisive test than by assembling converging lines of evidence against a firm case definition. Investigators fix the temporal and spatial boundaries of the event, characterise the exposures the animals shared, and then ask which candidate cause predicts the observed pattern of who died, where, and how quickly. Each candidate is treated as a hypothesis with determinate consequences for gross pathology, microbiology and toxicology, and the hypothesis whose consequences fail is discarded.",
